          <a href="#by-job" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Choosing by Joint</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          A caulk fills a gap that stays put. A sealant seals a joint that moves all year round. Those are genuinely different engineering problems with different answers, and the only reason the two words feel interchangeable is that manufacturers print whichever one they think will sell more of the tube.
        </p>

        <h2 id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. The Short Answer
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Caulk</strong>, used properly, means a relatively rigid gap filler. It cures hard, it is usually paintable, and it is designed for joints that stay still — the line where trim meets a wall, a gap left by an imperfect cut, a nail hole in moulding.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Sealant</strong> means an elastic material engineered for joints that move. It cures to a rubber, it stretches and compresses with the joint, and it is what you want anywhere temperature, moisture or building movement changes the width of the gap through the year.
        </p>

        <p>
          In the trade those categories are distinct and consequential. On a hardware store shelf they are not: silicone is universally sold as "silicone caulk" despite being a textbook sealant, and plenty of acrylic tubes carry the word "sealant" despite having almost no elasticity. Reading the noun on the front is close to useless.
        </p>

        <p>
          Which means the practical version of this question is not "caulk or sealant" but <strong>"how much does this joint move, and what does the tube say it can handle?"</strong>
        </p>

        <h2 id="movement" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Movement Is the Real Difference
        </h2>

        <p>
          Every joint in a building changes width. Materials expand in heat and contract in cold, timber swells with humidity, and the building itself settles and shifts. A half-inch joint between two materials with different expansion rates can easily change by a sixteenth of an inch between a summer afternoon and a winter night — which does not sound like much until you realise it is happening to a bead of material bonded rigidly to both sides.
        </p>

        <p>
          The specification that matters is <strong>movement capability</strong>, expressed as a percentage of joint width the cured product can accommodate. It usually appears as a figure such as ±25% or ±50%.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Under about ±10%</strong> is filler territory: acrylic latex, painter's caulk, most budget tubes. Fine in a static gap, and destined to crack in a moving one.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>±25%</strong> is the practical threshold for a real sealant. On a half-inch joint that is an eighth of an inch of movement in each direction, which covers the great majority of residential window, door and siding joints.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>±50%</strong> is high-performance territory — premium silicones and hybrid polymers, used where movement is severe or where failure is expensive to fix.
        </p>

        <p>
          A product with no published movement figure is telling you something by omission. Manufacturers who have tested to a standard print the number.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">±25%</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum for a real sealant</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2:1</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ideal width-to-depth ratio</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/4"</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Depth above which you need backer rod</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">3</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sides of adhesion that ruin a joint</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Working Out How Much Your Joint Moves</h3>
        <p>
          You do not need a calculation for most residential work, but a rough sense helps. Joints between two pieces of the same material in a sheltered position move least. Joints between dissimilar materials — timber to brick, metal to masonry, vinyl to render — move most, because the two sides expand at different rates in the same weather. A south-facing elevation in full sun swings through a far wider temperature range than a shaded north wall, and moves accordingly.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical rule: if the joint is outdoors, or joins two different materials, or sits in direct sun, treat it as a moving joint and buy accordingly. The cost of over-specifying is a few dollars; the cost of under-specifying is redoing the work.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-is-caulk" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. What Gets Called Caulk
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Acrylic latex and painter's caulk</strong> are the archetype: water-based, paintable, cheap, easy to tool, and almost rigid once cured. They exist to make an imperfect junction look intentional under a coat of paint, and at that they are excellent.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Siliconised acrylic</strong> sits just above, with a little silicone added for adhesion and water resistance. Still a filler in movement terms, still paintable, still the wrong choice in a shower or an exterior joint that flexes.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Butyl</strong> is the old-school outlier — sticky, durable, used on gutters, flashing and metal-to-metal joints. It is genuinely weatherproof, unpleasant to tool, and not paintable in any satisfying way.
        </p>

        <p>
          What unites the caulk end of the shelf is that these products are chosen for how they finish rather than how they perform under stress. That is a legitimate priority in the right place — interior trim work is mostly a cosmetic problem — but it is the wrong priority on the outside of a building.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-is-sealant" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. What Gets Called Sealant
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Silicone</strong> is the classic sealant: permanently elastic, waterproof, superb adhesion to glass, tile and metal, typically rated ±25% or better. Its cost is paintability — nothing sticks to it, including paint and including fresh silicone.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Polyurethane</strong> is the construction sealant: high movement capability, excellent adhesion to masonry and concrete, tough enough to take abrasion, and paintable. It is messier to work with and cures more slowly, and it is the standard answer for expansion joints and below-grade work. Our <a href="/polyurethane-vs-silicone-caulk/">polyurethane vs silicone comparison</a> covers that choice.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Hybrid polymers</strong> — MS polymer, silane-modified, "advanced polymer" — are the modern middle ground: much of silicone's elasticity and life, plus paintability and easier tooling, on a wide range of substrates. They cost more per tube and remove most of the compromises, which is why they increasingly dominate premium exterior work.
        </p>

        <p>
          The common thread is that these products are chosen for what the joint does over twenty years rather than for how the bead looks on the day.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Cure Type: The Other Thing Worth Knowing</h3>
        <p>
          Sealants cure in different ways, and it affects where they can be used more than most buyers realise.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Acetoxy silicone</strong> — the common, cheaper kind — releases acetic acid as it cures, which is the strong vinegar smell. That acid can corrode some metals and etch certain stone and mirror backing, so it belongs on glass, glazed tile and plastics rather than on marble, copper or a mirror edge.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Neutral cure silicone</strong> costs more and releases alcohol or oxime instead, making it safe on stone, metal and sensitive substrates. If the tube does not say neutral cure and the surface is natural stone or bare metal, assume acetoxy and choose differently.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Moisture-cure polyurethanes and hybrids</strong> pull moisture from the air, which means they cure slowly in very dry conditions and faster in humid ones. On a cold dry week, a polyurethane joint can stay soft for days longer than the label suggests — worth planning around rather than being surprised by.
        </p>

        <h2 id="labels" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Why the Labels Mislead
        </h2>

        <p>
          Three habits on packaging cause most of the confusion, and knowing them makes a shelf far easier to read.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>"Caulk" is used as a generic noun.</strong> In American usage particularly, anything in a cartridge is caulk, which is why silicone sealant is sold as silicone caulk. The word is describing the format, not the chemistry.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>"Sealant" is used as a marketing upgrade.</strong> Plenty of ordinary acrylic products carry it because it sounds more capable. Without a movement rating behind it, the word means nothing.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Names imply chemistry that is not there.</strong> "Siliconised" is the main offender — it signals silicone to a shopper while describing latex with a minor additive. "Paintable silicone" is similar: those are hybrids, because true silicone cannot be painted.
        </p>

        <p>
          The reliable tells are on the back. A movement rating, a cleanup instruction (water means acrylic; mineral spirits means silicone or solvent-borne), a stated warranty length, and a list of approved substrates will identify a product accurately in about fifteen seconds, whatever the front of the tube claims.
        </p>

        <h2 id="by-job" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Choosing by Joint
        </h2>

        <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
          <table class="w-full text-left">
            <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Joint</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Moves?</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interior trim to wall</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Barely</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acrylic latex caulk</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Exterior window perimeter</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, seasonally</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hybrid or silicone, ±25%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bath or shower perimeter</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, when filled</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silicone with mildewcide</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete expansion joint</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, significantly</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane, self-levelling</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Siding to masonry</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, dissimilar materials</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hybrid polymer</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gutter seams</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, thermal</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Butyl or silicone</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white">
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nail holes in moulding</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Painter's caulk or filler</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Whatever you choose, joint geometry matters as much as chemistry. A sealant bead should be roughly <strong>twice as wide as it is deep</strong>, which lets it stretch like a rubber band rather than tearing. That means backer rod in anything deeper than about a quarter inch — not to save material, but because a deep bead physically cannot flex.
        </p>

        <p>
          Backer rod also prevents <strong>three-sided adhesion</strong>, where the sealant bonds to the back of the joint as well as both faces. A bead stuck on three sides cannot move at all, and it tears itself apart the first time the joint opens. This single detail separates professional caulking from the version that fails in two years.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">How Long Each Category Lasts</h3>
        <p>
          Service life follows directly from the movement question, which is why the two are worth considering together.
        </p>
        <p>
          A rigid filler in a static interior joint can last as long as the paint over it — a decade or more, because nothing is stressing it. The same product in a moving exterior joint often fails within one or two seasons, not because it weathered badly but because it was pulled apart.
        </p>
        <p>
          A quality sealant in a properly formed joint should last fifteen to twenty-five years depending on chemistry and exposure. When one fails early, the cause is almost always geometry or preparation rather than the product: a bead too deep to stretch, adhesion on three sides, or a surface that was dusty or damp when the bead went on.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the practical reason the caulk-versus-sealant question matters at all. The price difference between the two categories is a few dollars per tube, while the difference in how often you redo the work can be a factor of ten.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by the Word on the Front</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                "Caulk" and "sealant" are used interchangeably in marketing. The movement rating on the back is the specification that predicts whether the bead survives, and a tube without one is usually a filler.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a Moving Joint With a Rigid Product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Painter's caulk around an exterior window looks perfect for one season and splits along one edge in the first cold snap. Movement, not weather, is what kills it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Backer Rod</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A deep bead cannot stretch, and one bonded to the back of the joint cannot move at all. Rod sets the depth and breaks that third bond — it is the cheapest component in the job and the one most often left out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Expensive Means Flexible</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some premium products are premium for adhesion, colour range or cure speed rather than elasticity. Check the percentage rather than the price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over a Dirty or Damp Joint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No chemistry adheres to dust, old flaking sealant or a wet substrate. Preparation determines the outcome far more than the difference between two good products.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Verdict -->
        <div id="verdict" class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-4 py-2 rounded-bl-xl">
            ★ Our Verdict
          </div>
          <h2 id="the-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">Caulk vs Sealant: The Verdict</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#38BDF8]">Use a caulk if:</strong> the joint is static, interior, and destined to be painted. Trim, mouldings, nail holes and the gaps that are purely cosmetic all want a cheap, paintable, easily tooled filler — and a premium sealant there is money wasted on a harder job.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#FF4500]">Use a sealant if:</strong> the joint moves, gets wet, sits outdoors, or joins two different materials. Look for ±25% movement capability as the minimum, and treat anything without a published rating as a filler regardless of what the label calls it.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
              <strong class="text-white">The Honest Bottom Line:</strong> Stop reading the noun on the front and start reading the number on the back. The distinction between caulk and sealant is real, but it lives in the movement rating rather than in the product name — and once you are checking that figure, the shelf sorts itself out in seconds. Then get the geometry right: backer rod, twice as wide as deep, and never bonded on three sides.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between caulk and sealant?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Elasticity. Caulk cures relatively rigid and fills gaps that barely move; sealant stays flexible and is engineered for joints that expand and contract. The words are used interchangeably on packaging, so read the movement rating rather than the noun.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is silicone a caulk or a sealant?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Technically a sealant — permanently flexible and rated for high movement. It is sold everywhere as "silicone caulk", which is the clearest example of the two terms being used for the same product regardless of chemistry.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which for windows and doors?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">A sealant, because those joints move seasonally. Look for at least ±25% movement capability — silicone or a hybrid polymer. Painter's caulk is fine on the interior trim line but not on the exterior joint where frame meets wall.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use caulk instead of sealant?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Only in a joint that does not move. In a static gap a rigid caulk is adequate and easier to paint. In a moving joint it cracks or pulls away from one side, usually at the first significant temperature swing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What does ±25% mean?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">The cured bead can stretch and compress by a quarter of the joint's width without failing — an eighth of an inch each way on a half-inch joint. Ratings of ±25% to ±50% indicate a genuine sealant; single digits or no figure indicate a filler.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is painter's caulk a sealant?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. It is acrylic latex for filling small interior gaps and disappearing under paint, with minimal movement capability and limited water resistance — right for trim, wrong for anything weathered or flexing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do sealants need backer rod?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">In any joint deeper than about a quarter inch, yes. It sets the bead depth to roughly half the width so it can stretch, and prevents three-sided adhesion, where sealant bonds to the back of the joint and cannot flex at all.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealant cost more?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Usually, by a few dollars a tube. Since failure means cutting out the old bead and doing the job again, paying that difference on any joint that moves is the cheaper decision over time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

