        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Caulk fills, sealant flexes.</strong> The distinction is real and the packaging ignores it, so buy on the movement rating instead: under ±10% is a filler for static interior gaps, ±25% or better is a sealant for anything that moves, gets wet, or lives outdoors.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Then treat the joint as seriously as the product. Backer rod in anything deep, a bead about twice as wide as it is deep, adhesion on two sides only, and a clean dry surface underneath. A mid-priced sealant installed that way will comfortably outlast a premium one squeezed into a dirty three-sided joint.
          </p>
